From f163e30be39098d5ab01d85d74b5d54d65f3376a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zahary Karadjov <zahary@schellingpointlabs.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 16:26:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Never forget an instance the provider may still hold; stop
 delete storms

Four related pool-manager defects let instances leak or let a single
unreachable provider host stall or storm the pool manager.

1. cleanupOrphanedGithubRunners: a runner that is offline in the forge and
   absent from the provider's ListInstances had its database record deleted
   outright; the provider's DeleteInstance was never called. "Absent from
   the list" is only the provider's claim, and a provider that cannot see a
   class of instance (or answers an enumeration failure with an empty list)
   makes it about instances that are still running, which then leak for
   good. Mark the instance pending_delete instead, so the normal deletion
   path calls DeleteInstance (idempotent for an instance that is really
   gone) before the record is removed. Count these events in
   garm_runner_absent_from_provider_total{provider}.

2. retryFailedInstancesForOnePool ran a pool's cleanup deletes in one
   errgroup.WithContext. The first failing delete cancelled every sibling
   delete mid-flight (external providers are killed on cancellation) and
   aborted the pass, and everything was retried on the next 5s tick. Run
   them in a plain errgroup, never return a per-instance failure, and back
   each failing instance off with the existing instance backoff.

3. A create that fails fast (e.g. a full storage pool) was re-queued on
   every 5s tick, exhausting maxCreateAttempts in seconds. Wait
   30s * 2^(attempt-1), capped at 20 minutes, since the instance last
   changed before re-queueing it. The per-instance delete backoff entry is
   dropped when the delete succeeds, when the instance runs out of create
   attempts, and when its record is deleted, so the in-memory map does not
   grow for the life of the process.

4. cleanupOrphanedGithubRunners returned on the first pool whose
   ListInstances failed, skipping every other pool of the entity on every
   pass: one unreachable provider host stopped the orphan sweep (and fix 1)
   for all of them. Skip only the failing pool, keep sweeping the rest, and
   report the skipped pools once the pass is done.

Signed-off-by: Zahary Karadjov <zahary@schellingpointlabs.com>
